extends RefCounted
## The words of the parts guide (parts_guide.gd): a page for every part and
## for each general topic. A part's settings, inputs and outputs are listed
## from part_settings.gd and part_ports.gd, so they never go out of date;
## these pages say what the part does, how to use it and where its limits
## are. BBCode, as RichTextLabel reads it.

const SHAPE := "A plain shape. It has no behaviour of its own: it adds mass (its real volume × its material's density) and strength (health = mass × the material's strength), and it welds to every part it shares a face with or overlaps. Parts of the same material may overlap; parts of different materials may not.\n\nResize it in the Config tool in steps of 0.005 block (1.25 mm). Its collision is its true shape, so it rolls, slides and floats as it looks."

const PARTS := {
	"block": "The basic building block: a solid box. Most builds are mostly blocks.",
	"wedge": "A block cut corner to corner along one face: a ramp. Good for noses, fairings and ramps.",
	"triangle_wedge": "A wedge whose sloping face is also cut back to a point: a triangular prism.",
	"cylinder": "A round cylinder along its own Y axis. Rolls on its side. A plain cylinder does not turn by itself: put it on a motor, bearing or wheel axle.",
	"sphere": "A ball. Rolls in every direction, so a build resting on spheres has almost no grip sideways.",
	"slab": "A half-height block: floors, decks and thin walls.",
	"plate": "A quarter-height block: skins, panels and floors where weight matters.",
	"post": "A thin square column, half a block across.",
	"rod": "A thin round rod, a quarter block across. Light, and good for struts and axles you only need to look at.",
	"corner_wedge": "A corner cut off a block: one sixth of its volume. For rounding off the corners of a hull.",
	"inverse_corner": "A block with one corner cut away: the inside of a rounded corner.",
	"pyramid": "A square pyramid, point up along +Y.",
	"cone": "A round cone, point up along +Y. Noses, spinners and nozzles to look at.",
	"frustum": "A cylinder that narrows toward its top.",
	"hemisphere": "Half a ball, flat face down. Domes and canopies.",
	"half_cylinder": "A cylinder cut along its length.",
	"convex_curve": "A quarter round: the outside of a curve.",
	"concave_curve": "A block with a quarter round scooped out: the inside of a curve.",
	"arch": "A block with a round arch through it. Its collision follows the opening, so things fit through.",
	"ring": "A flat ring. The hole is open.",
	"pipe": "A tube along Y. The hole is open, but a pipe shape carries no fluid: for that use the Fluid pipe (Fluid and Engine tab).",
	"torus": "A ring with a round section, like a tyre to look at. It is not a wheel: for wheels use the Wheel.",

	"motor": "Turns its top (the side with the plate) about its own +Y against its housing, at up to its max speed with up to its max torque, speeding up at its acceleration. Whatever touches the top face turns with it; the housing welds to everything else.\n\nDrive it with Clockwise or Counterclockwise (on/off, from input blocks) or Speed (a number from −1 to 1, a share of max speed). With no input it holds still, or spins freely if set to. Outputs its shaft speed in rpm.\n\n[b]Limits.[/b] The physics engine holds the speed as part of solving the joint, so a motor is very stiff. A motor turning a heavy load needs enough torque or it stalls. The two bodies it joins do not collide with each other.",
	"servo": "Turns its top about its own +Y to an angle and holds it there rigidly. Go to positive target and Go to negative target (on/off) send it to its two set angles; Angle (a number of degrees) sends it anywhere. With neither it goes back to its default angle, or holds where it is if set to. It moves at its speed, easing in and out. Angles are 0 as built; positive is counterclockwise looking down on the top. Outputs its angle.\n\nThe keys take priority over Angle, so you can fly on a PID through Angle and take over with keys.\n\n[b]Limits.[/b] For control surfaces under an autopilot, raise the speed (300°/s or more) so it keeps up.",
	"door_hinge_servo": "A servo shaped like a door hinge: an upright leaf swings about the edge it shares with its base plate. Works exactly like the servo (same settings and inputs). Good for doors, flaps, ramps and landing-gear doors.",
	"hinge": "A free pin joint along its own X axis: the top half swings against the base half. Each half welds to what touches it; something touching both welds to the side it shares more area with.\n\nSettings: angle limits (off by default, so it turns freely) and friction torque. Outputs its angle.\n\n[b]Limits.[/b] The two bodies a joint joins do not collide, so use the angle limits to stop it. If both halves end up welded to the same body through other parts, it cannot turn: the spawn message counts locked joints.",
	"ball_joint": "The top swivels in every direction on the base. Settings: an angle limit (largest tilt from straight), and \"carries rotation\", which stops it twisting so it passes turning through like a universal joint.",
	"bearing": "A housing that welds by its four sides, and a shaft through it that welds by its two ends (along its own Y). The shaft turns freely against its friction torque. Use it to carry axles, gears and propellers. Outputs the shaft speed in rpm.",
	"piston": "A barrel and a rod end that slides out along its own +Y. Extend (on/off) runs it out to its target length; Length (a number of blocks) sends it anywhere from closed to its longest reach. It moves at its speed with easing, pushing with up to its max force, and holds when idle (or returns to its starting length, if set). Outputs its length in blocks.\n\n[b]Limits.[/b] Current length sets how far out it is as built. With \"Sensor mode\" on, it takes no command and becomes a load cell: it holds its built length with a very stiff spring and puts out the pressure on it (force over its cross-section, kPa, positive when pushed in): weigh things, or feel a landing gear's load.",
	"solenoid": "A short, fast actuator: On snaps the top out by its stroke with its set force, and off pulls it back. Outputs whether it is out.",
	"spring": "Two plates pushed apart when shorter than the rest length and pulled together when longer: force = stiffness × stretch, less damping × speed. The coils stretch to match.\n\n[b]Limits.[/b] A force worked out once a tick cannot be infinitely stiff: very stiff springs on light parts are softened automatically so they do not shake apart.",
	"gear": "A spur gear, fixed to whatever it touches (mount it on a motor, bearing or wheel axle so it can turn). Two gears turn together, exactly in the ratio of their teeth, when their shafts are parallel, they sit level with each other and their rims touch. A gear sitting on a rack's teeth drives the rack along.\n\n[b]Limits.[/b] The gearing is exact maths, not tooth collisions: gears that do not meet those three conditions do not mesh, however close they look. A train whose ratios disagree round a loop jams, and the spawn message warns about it.",
	"bevel_gear": "Like a gear, but for shafts at right angles: two bevel gears whose shafts cross at 90° and whose rims touch turn together in the ratio of their teeth.",
	"rack": "A straight toothed bar. A gear sitting on its teeth with its shaft across the rack drives it along (or is driven by it). Set the teeth per block to match the gear's spacing.",
	"pulley": "A belt pulley. Pulleys with the same belt number, parallel shafts and level with each other turn together in the ratio of their sizes, however far apart, and the belt is drawn between them. Belt 0 means no belt.",
	"crankshaft": "Journals that weld by the two ends (along its own X), with webs and a crank pin that turn between them. Join a linkage bar from the crank pin to a piston rod or a combustion cylinder's rod to turn sliding into turning, or turning into sliding. Outputs its angle (counting whole turns) and speed in rpm.\n\nThe throw radius and rod length describe the crank and rod you build, and drive two more outputs: Rod end position, the distance from the crank's axis to the far end of the rod, r cos θ + √(L² − r² sin² θ) (greatest, top dead centre, as built); and Near the top, on within 15° either side of top dead centre: link it to a combustion cylinder's Spark for simple ignition timing.",
	"wheel": "Set to spin freely (the default), the hub welds by the ends of its axle (its own X) and the round tyre turns on it, against its hub friction. Turn spin freely off to make a solid wheel to bolt onto a motor's top. Outputs its speed in rpm.\n\n[b]Limits.[/b] Grip comes from the tyre's material: rubber grips best.",
	"linkage_bar": "A bar with a joint at each end: ball joints, or hinges about Y or Z, as set. Each end welds to what touches it. Crank-and-rod mechanisms, steering linkages, landing-gear struts.",
	"clutch": "Two plates that turn together with up to the max torque and slip past it. Engage (on/off) or Amount (0 to 1, a share of the max torque) controls it; with neither it is engaged or not as set.",
	"brake": "A caliper that welds by its +X face and a disc that welds by its two ends (its own Y). Brake (on/off) or Amount (0 to 1) holds the disc with up to the max torque.",

	"light_panel": "Glows in its color while On is on (always, if On is not linked), and lights up what is around it. Red, Green and Blue inputs (0 to 255) override the color while linked; brightness sets how strong.\n\n[b]Limits.[/b] Only the first 24 light panels on a build light their surroundings; the rest still glow.",
	"vanish_block": "Disappears and stops being solid while Vanish is on. With \"Comes back when the signal ends\" off, it stays gone once it has vanished. It keeps its mass.",
	"impact_detector": "Puts out the acceleration it feels in g (0 at rest or in steady flight, not counting gravity), and switches Triggered on for its signal length whenever that passes its trigger level. Link Triggered to explosives for impact fuzes, or to logic.",
	"input_block": "Turns a key into a signal, while you sit in a seat. Hold: on while the key is down. Toggle: each press flips it. \"Number from two keys\" puts out +1 for the first key, −1 for the second and 0 for neither, for throttles, steering and motors. Outputs Pressed (on/off) and Value (number).\n\nType the key's name in Key: a letter, a digit, or a name Godot knows, such as Space, Shift, Up, Left, Kp 1.",
	"boolean_gate": "On/off logic. AND, OR, NOT (of A), XOR, NAND, NOR. Latch: A switches it on and B switches it off. Timer: when A switches on, the result stays on for the timer length. Any number other than 0 counts as on.",
	"number_gate": "Arithmetic on numbers. An unlinked B uses the Constant setting, so Add with only A linked adds the constant. Constant, Add, Subtract (A − B), Multiply, Divide (0 if B is 0), Absolute value, Min, Max, Clamp (A between B and C), Compare (on if A > B), Select (B if A is on, else C), Map range (A from B…C onto 0…1), Modulo (A wrapped into 0 up to B: a crank's angle into one turn) and Between (on while A is between B and C: a spark window).\n\n\"Comparison is true\" is on whenever the result is not 0 (or, for Compare and Between, when the test passes).",
	"pid_controller": "Drives a measurement toward a setpoint: output = Kp × error + Ki × its running total + Kd × its rate, where error = setpoint − measurement, held between the lowest and highest output. The running total stops growing while the output is pinned at a limit, and the rate comes from the measurement, so a jump in the setpoint does not jolt it.\n\n[b]Tuning.[/b] Start with Kp only. If it runs away instead of correcting, make all three gains negative. Raise Kp until it holds but oscillates, add Kd (about a third of Kp) to calm it, then a little Ki (about a tenth) to take out a steady offset. Cascade two for altitude hold: altitude PID → its output is the pitch PID's setpoint.",
	"pwm_generator": "A square wave: Output is on for the duty cycle's share of every cycle and off for the rest, at its frequency. Duty (0 to 1) and Frequency (Hz) inputs override the settings while linked; Enable (on if not linked) stops it with the output off. Phase puts out where it is in the cycle, 0 to 1, as a sawtooth.\n\nUses: pulse a thruster or light; link a PID's output to Duty to throttle something that is only on or off.\n\n[b]Limits.[/b] Up to 120 Hz: physics runs 240 steps a second, so at high frequencies the duty is rounded to whole steps (at 60 Hz, quarters).",
	"speed_sensor": "Puts out how fast the point it sits on is moving, in m/s, including the spin of its body.",
	"altitude_sensor": "Puts out its height above sea level in metres. The lake is at 881 m and the build pad at 901 m.",
	"attitude_sensor": "Puts out pitch (nose up positive), roll (right side down positive) and yaw (compass heading: 0 north, 90 east) in degrees, and each one's rate in degrees per second. All taken from the sensor's own forward (−Z) and up (+Y): mount it with its blue Z arrow pointing back toward the tail and green Y up.",
	"distance_sensor": "Looks straight ahead along its own −Z and puts out the distance in metres to the first part, building, ground or water, up to its range; it reads the range if nothing is there. Uses: landing-height readouts, collision warnings, radar altimeters (point it down).",

	"explosive_block": "Goes off when Detonate switches on, or when it is destroyed. Every part within the blast radius takes 1000 HP per metre of radius at the centre, falling to nothing at the edge, and is pushed away; buildings and wizard tower blocks in range are damaged too. An explosive caught in a blast goes off one tick later, so chains of them ripple.",
	"machine_gun": "Fires along its own −Z while Fire is on, at its fire rate. Each round is an instant line out to its range doing 4 HP to the first thing it hits (parts, buildings, tower blocks), with a small push and recoil. With unlimited ammo off, link Ammo supply to an ammo generator set to machine gun rounds.",
	"large_cannon": "Fires a shell along its own −Z when Fire is on. Its size sets the shot: the bore is a fifth of its smaller cross size, the shell three bores long, made of the projectile material, and pushed down the barrel at 100 MPa, so muzzle energy = 100 MPa × bore area × barrel length (Config shows it). Shells fly under gravity and do 1 HP per kJ they carry; a shell goes through whatever it destroys, slowed by the energy that took. One shot per 0.5 s per metre of barrel, with recoil.",
	"ammo_generator": "Makes rounds or shells at its rate up to its storage, starting empty, and gets heavier as it fills (25 g a round; a shell weighs what the heaviest linked cannon fires). Turn off a gun's unlimited ammo and link its Ammo supply here.",

	"fluid_cylinder": "A barrel and rod like a piston's, moved by fluid. [b]Hydraulic oil:[/b] the pumps in its network push the rod out at the pumps' flow over the cylinders' area (back in when a pump's Rate is negative) with up to the pump pressure × its area; with the pumps stopped the oil locks and it holds (up to 20 MPa). [b]Gas:[/b] it shares one pressure with the pressure tanks and gas cylinders in its network and pushes with (pressure − outside air) × area: an air spring.\n\nOutputs pressure (kPa) and length (blocks). It must touch the pump or tank (or a pipe that does) to be in their network.",
	"pump": "Moves fluid at its max flow × Rate (−1 to 1; full if Rate is not linked) while On is on (always, if On is not linked), up to its max pressure. In a hydraulic network it drives the cylinders; in a gas network it pumps outside air in (or out, backward); in a fuel network it feeds combustion cylinders, so its Rate is the throttle. Outputs its flow in L/s.",
	"valve": "Joins the network on both sides while open and splits it while closed. Open (on/off), Amount (over 0 is open) or \"Open with no input\".",
	"fluid_pipe": "Carries fluid between the parts it touches: anything touching a pipe is in its network. Run pipes to put a tank far from the engine.",
	"fuel_tank": "Holds its volume in fuel (litres = its size in m³ × 1000), starting at its fill. Petrol by default: 34 MJ/L and 0.75 kg/L; change them for other fuels. It weighs a light shell (15% of a solid block) plus its fuel, so it gets lighter as fuel burns and the center of mass moves. Outputs its level in %. Anything that burns fuel draws it from the tanks in its network.",
	"pressure_tank": "A volume of gas at its starting pressure (kPa, absolute; 101.3 is outside air). It shares its pressure with the gas cylinders in its network: a reservoir for air springs and gas rams. Outputs its pressure in kPa.",
	"combustion_cylinder": "The heart of a piston engine you build yourself. Join its rod to a crankshaft's pin with a linkage bar, have a pump and fuel tank in its network (touching it, or by pipes), and turn the crank to start it (a motor makes a starter).\n\nEach cycle: the pump sends fuel in, at most what one cylinder of air can burn. It fires by itself just past the top of its stroke, or when Spark switches on if Spark is linked. The fuel burns at once (14.7 kg of air per kg of fuel), the pressure jumps, and the gas expands as the rod goes out, pushing the crank round. Near the bottom it breathes out and in, and squeezes the fresh air 9 to 1 on the way back up. Outputs its pressure in kPa.\n\n[b]Limits.[/b] Peak pressures are real, around 10 to 15 MPa: a 0.6-block cylinder pushes its rod with hundreds of kN for an instant. Use solid steel for the crank and rods, and expect to tune. A 0.6 × 0.6 block cylinder burns about 0.3 mL a firing: set the pump's max flow to about 0.02 L/s per cylinder.",
	"compressor": "One of the four parts of a jet engine (with a combustor, turbine and nozzle, all in one network with a fuel tank). Its face area sets how much air the engine swallows (air density × area × 100 m/s at full spool) and its pressure ratio sets the efficiency (higher is better: 8 is a 1950s jet, 30 a modern one). Outputs the spool speed in rpm.",
	"combustor": "Burns fuel in a jet: its max fuel flow × Fuel rate (0 to 1; full if not linked), drawn from the tanks in its network. Fuel rate is the jet's throttle. The spool winds up over a few seconds to match.",
	"turbine": "Turns the jet's compressor: a jet with no turbine has nothing to drive its spool and makes no thrust. Outputs the spool speed in rpm.",
	"nozzle": "Where a jet's thrust comes out: it pushes along its own −Z (the exhaust goes out of its +Z), with thrust = air flow × the speed the jet gains. Thrust falls as you fly faster. A compressor 4 blocks across with 0.1 L/s of fuel gives about 16 kN standing still; 1 L/s about 50 kN. Outputs its thrust in N.",
	"thruster": "Pushes along its own −Z with its set thrust, from the moment of spawning unless On is linked; Throttle (−1 to 1) scales it and runs it backward. With \"Runs without fuel\" off it burns fuel from its network (about 0.5 L/s per 1000 N) and stops when the tanks are dry. Outputs its thrust.",
	"solid_rocket": "Lights when Ignite switches on and pushes along its own −Z with its thrust for its burn time, getting lighter as its propellant burns (thrust × time ÷ 2000 m/s), then goes out for good. \"Never burns out\" keeps it going.",

	"aero_panel": "A wing surface. It takes the air's speed at its centre (including its body's spin), and makes lift = ½ × air density × speed² × area × lift coefficient at right angles to the airflow, and drag along it. Lift coefficient 4.0 per radian up to the 15° stall, flat plate beyond. Its flat faces look along its own Y.\n\n[b]Limits.[/b] A flat panel makes no lift at zero angle of attack: give wings a few degrees of incidence. The Config/Transform tools show the center of lift (blue ball) at a 5° angle of attack: keep it just behind the center of mass (yellow) for a stable plane.",
	"aero_panel_triangle": "A triangular wing surface: the same as the square panel with half the area. Delta wings and tapered tips.",
	"aero_panel_dragless": "The same lift as the square panel with no drag at all. An idealised surface for testing and for builds that should not slow down.",
	"aero_panel_triangle_dragless": "The triangle panel with no drag.",
	"propeller": "On a motor or bearing, pulls along its own spin axis (Y) by its speed, blade count and pitch: the pitch sets how far each turn screws it forward, and thrust falls as the incoming air catches up with that. Spun the other way it pushes the other way. It loads the motor with the torque it takes. Outputs its speed in rpm.\n\n[b]Limits.[/b] The default size is tiny (one block across): stretch it to several blocks for real thrust (8 blocks = 2 m).",
	"lift_gas_tank": "Lifts straight up by its setting per block of its size, scaled by Amount (0 to 1; full if not linked), weakening as the air thins with height.",

	"seat": "Where you sit to drive: walk up and press E; Space stands you up. Keys bound in input blocks work only while you sit in a seat. Outputs Occupied (on while you sit in it): link it to switch things on when someone climbs in.",
	"anchor_block": "Fixes its whole body in place when spawned: a solid base for test rigs, cranes and turrets on the ground.",
	"graph_display": "A screen that plots up to four signals against time, so you can see what your logic is doing: link Line A to D to anything (a sensor, a PID's output, a gate). Each line has its own colour (A yellow, B cyan, C pink, D green) and the name you give it.\n\nThe picture shows on both big faces of the part. While you sit in the vehicle, it is also drawn on the left of the screen, bigger, with each line's latest value (turn that off with \"Also show on screen\"). Up to three screens show there at once.\n\nThe scale fits itself to the lines unless you set a bottom and a top. Time shown sets how many seconds scroll across. Tuning a PID: graph the setpoint (A) and the measurement (B) together, and watch the overshoot shrink as you raise Kd.",
	"magnet": "Grabs whatever loose body touches its pole face (its own +Y: the steel face) while On is on, and holds it rigidly: other vehicles, crates, wreckage. With \"Grabs the ground\" on it grabs the ground and buildings too, to anchor a crane or a tow. It lets go when switched off, or when holding on would take more than its holding force (worked out from how hard the held thing is being shaken about, as well as its weight). Holding (on/off) says when it has something.\n\nOn with no input makes it grab as soon as it spawns, which is handy for a magnet on a crane hook.",
	"winch": "A drum with a rope and hook. The rope comes out of its bottom face (its own −Y) to a hook hanging on it, its starting length below. Reel (a number: +1 winds in, −1 lets out, at the reel speed) changes the length, between half a metre and the longest rope. The rope only pulls when taut, like a real one: a stiff spring tuned to the masses at its two ends, damped so loads do not bounce.\n\nThe hook grabs any loose body it touches (not the build it hangs from) while Hook grabs is on (always, with no input, by default), and lets go when it switches off. Past its breaking tension the rope snaps. Outputs the rope length, the tension in newtons and whether the hook is holding something.\n\nGood for: cranes, helicopter sling loads, towing, and picking up cargo-run crates.",
	"coupler": "Locks to another coupler when their cones (their −Z faces) meet face to face, within about a third of a metre, while both want to couple (Couple on, or \"Couples with no input\"). It works between different vehicles: trains, tow bars, trailers, docking ports, mid-air refuelling. Switch Couple off on either one to come apart.\n\nSet one side to \"Give fuel\" (or \"Take fuel\") and, while coupled, fuel flows between the two builds' fuel tanks at its flow rate. Outputs whether it is coupled and the litres moved so far.",
	"dynamic_offsetter": "Puts parts off the grid. At spawn, everything built on its top face (its own +Y), and everything joined on beyond that, is turned about the middle of that face by the turn settings and moved by the shift settings (up to 5 blocks), both along the offsetter's own axes, and held there, welded to the offsetter, however far it moved. Once spawned the offsetter itself is invisible and has no collision. Use it for dihedral and sweep on wings, canted engines, angled armour.\n\n[b]Limits.[/b] What is on top must not also reach round to touch another face of the offsetter, or it cannot be told apart from the rest and nothing moves. What is moved still welds to whatever it ends up touching.",
}

const TOPICS := [
	["Getting started", "[b]Build, spawn, drive.[/b] Pick a part from the tabs on the left (1 is the Place tool), click on the pad to place it, R / T / Y to turn it. Press Enter or the green Spawn button to set the build loose, and again (Build) to put it back exactly as it was.\n\n[b]Seats and keys.[/b] Every vehicle needs a seat to drive it. Link motor, servo and thruster inputs to input blocks in the Config tool (3): select the part, press Link beside an input, then click the part the signal comes from. Keys work while you sit in the seat (E to sit).\n\n[b]Check the numbers.[/b] Config shows each part's volume, mass and health. The yellow ball is the center of mass; the blue ball the center of lift."],
	["Spawn and welding", "Parts whose boxes share a face (or part of one) or overlap are welded into one rigid body; parts that only meet along an edge or at a corner are not. Separate groups become separate bodies.\n\nMachine parts (motors, hinges, pistons, cylinders and so on) come apart into pieces joined by a real joint, and each piece welds to what touches it. Something touching two pieces of one machine welds to the one it shares more area with. If both sides of a joint weld to the same body through other parts, the joint is locked: the spawn message counts locked joints.\n\nThe two bodies a joint connects do not collide with each other."],
	["Signals and logic", "On/off signals are 1 and 0; any number other than 0 counts as on. An unlinked input uses its default (each part's page says what). Signals pass through a chain of up to four gates in the same physics tick (1/240 s); longer chains take a tick per extra four.\n\nLogic parts run only once the build is spawned. Keys from input blocks work while you are seated."],
	["Fluids and engines", "Fluid parts that touch face to face join into a network; a closed valve splits it. What a network does depends on what is in it: fuel (tanks feed combustors, combustion cylinders through a pump, and fuel-fed thrusters), hydraulic oil (pumps drive hydraulic cylinders) or gas (pressure tanks and gas cylinders share one pressure).\n\nA jet engine is a compressor, combustor, turbine and nozzle in one network with a fuel tank. A piston engine is combustion cylinders, a crankshaft, linkage bars, a pump and a tank."],
	["Flying", "Lift comes from aero panels only. Keep the center of lift (blue) a little behind the center of mass (yellow); give the wings a few degrees of incidence; use servos on tail panels for pitch and yaw and on wing panels for roll. Air thins with height, so planes need more speed up high.\n\nAn attitude sensor and a PID make a simple autopilot: see the PID controller's page."],
	["Water", "Spawned builds float by Archimedes: each part displaces its own volume, and air sealed inside a closed hull floats it too (found when you spawn, in quarter-block cells). A hull that loses a part floods. You can swim (Space to swim up)."],
	["Damage and destruction", "Every part has health = mass × material strength. A part whose health runs out disappears, and its body breaks into separate pieces if it is no longer joined.\n\nTown buildings have health for the space they enclose (2 HP/m³ wood, 4 brick, 6 concrete) and fall to rubble. Wizard towers are built of stone blocks (24 round, 18 high); a ring that loses half its blocks brings everything above it down. All world damage is put back when you press Build.\n\n[b]Crash damage.[/b] A body whose speed changes by more than 20 g, averaged over 50 ms, damages every part in it by that part's mass (0.05 HP per kg per g over, per tick): glass and lead break first, Kevlar and carbon fibre last. Switch it off in Settings → World."],
	["The map", "Flathead Lake, Montana, at true size: 29 by 50 km inside the wall. Real towns from OpenStreetMap, airports on the islands, by the towns and high in the mountains, an aircraft carrier somewhere on the lake, and six wizard towers in new places each time. Press M for the full map; click it to set a waypoint."],
	["The fleet", "The Fleet button at the top spawns any saved build out into the world, a few metres in front of you and facing the way you face, as a vehicle of its own. It stays, working, while you build something else on the pad, and you can sit in its seats like any other. Despawn vehicles one at a time or all at once from the same window. Up to 8 at a time. Fleet vehicles show as green squares on the maps. A vehicle (or the pad's build) more than 3 km from you is parked: frozen where it is, keeping its speed, and set going again when you come within 2.5 km.\n\nOnly the pad's Build button puts damaged buildings and towers back."],
	["Air drag", "Every body feels the air: for each of its own three axes, its outline seen along that axis (in quarter-block cells) catches the air with a drag coefficient of 1.0, acting at the middle of that outline. So a body with more outline behind its centre of mass turns its nose into the wind, like a dart. Aero panels and propellers are left out of the outline; they work out their own drag. A body's inertia comes from the real mass and place of each of its parts, and parts of one material that overlap count their shared volume once.\n\n[b]Friction[/b] between two surfaces is the geometric mean of their coefficients, √(μ1 μ2), against the ground (taken as 1): rubber on the ground grips at 1.05, ice at 0.22. Between two spawned bodies the engine takes the lower of their √μ."],
	["Health, crashes and the replay", "You have 100 health. Hard knocks hurt: a change of speed faster than 20 g, averaged over 50 ms, drains it (a fall of about 20 m or a crash at about 25 m/s hurts badly; a fall of 60 m or a crash at 35 m/s kills). So does a long, hard turn past 20 g, a blast close by, lava, a lava bomb. Health comes back slowly after 6 seconds unhurt. Settings → World can turn it off.\n\nThe last 30 seconds are always being recorded. Press 0 once something is spawned to watch them from a camera that follows what you were riding: drag to turn it, wheel to zoom, Restart, Speed (1x, 2x, 0.25x, 0.5x), and Skip (or Space, or Esc). A crash that takes 35 health while you are riding plays it on its own, and so does dying, after which you come back at the hangar. Dying ends any race as a loss."],
	["Stress view and breaking", "Press 9 while spawned to colour every part by how hard it is loaded: blue easy, then green, yellow and red at its breaking point. The readout on the left shows the most loaded part.\n\nHow it is worked out: each part carries its weight and its share of the body's acceleration (including turning), plus thrust, lift and contact forces put straight on it. The parts form a tree out from the one nearest the centre of mass, and each passes its load, and everything beyond it, through the face it shares with the next: stress = force ÷ area + bending moment ÷ section modulus (a square of that area). Strength is each material's real yield or breaking strength (balsa 10 MPa, wood 40, aluminium 270, steel 400, titanium 880, carbon fibre 600, Kevlar 1,500).\n\nWith structural failure on (Settings → World), a part past its strength for two checks in a row breaks off, and everything it held goes with it: wings snap at the root in a hard pull-up. Brace them, use stronger materials, or make the joint wider."],
	["Weather, seasons and disasters", "The Weather button opens the window for all of this.\n\nWeather: clear, cloudy, rain, thunderstorm, snow or fog, changing every few minutes on Auto. Wind blows with gusts, stronger higher up, and pushes on everything that flies, every body's outline and you: take off into it, land into it, and expect crosswinds. Waves on the lake grow with the wind (about 1 m at 10 m/s) and boats ride them.\n\nSeasons: a year lasts 10 game days (3 h 20 min). The snow line comes down from the peaks in autumn to the lake in midwinter; the grass turns gold in autumn and some trees yellow.\n\nDisasters, from the window or (Settings → World) on their own every 20 to 40 minutes: a tornado that throws cars and smashes buildings in its path; a hurricane (four minutes of 46 m/s winds); a tsunami (a 14 m wave from an earthquake under the lake that carries boats and runs up the shore); an earthquake; and the volcano, the tallest mountain (west of Lakeside), which throws glowing bombs kilometres, sends rivers of lava down its slopes and darkens the sky with ash. Calm everything stops them all."],
	["Races, cargo and cash", "Walk up to the people under the signs at the airports and press E. Air races (the Rookie Loop at Wild Horse Island at 100 m/s, then 200, 300, 400 and 500 m/s at Polson, Bigfork, Somers Mountain and Lakeside Ridge airports): pick one of your saved builds and you start on the runway in it against three rivals, through the rings in order and back over the airport. The next ring is yellow with a beam over it; the last is green. Car races start beside the highway in Polson, Bigfork and Lakeside.\n\nRivals show off when you come close, and speed up for a few seconds. Top three win money (half for second, a quarter for third); your best times are kept. F2 gives up.\n\nCargo runs: every airport has crates to deliver to any other. Get the crate there however you like (a cargo bay, a magnet, a winch hook) and set it down gently in the green ring. Pay by distance and weight, half again for a quick delivery; a hard knock smashes the crate."],
	["Caves", "Six caves: on Wild Horse Island (1.4 km south-east of the hangar), two in the Mission Range, one in the Salish hills west of the lake, one above Big Arm and one on the ridge above Somers. Each is a tunnel 28 m wide from a hillside down into a cavern about 340 m across and 120 m high, and from there a passage on down into a deeper cavern lit by glowing crystals, as much as 250 m below the entrance. They are on the map. Down there the daylight goes and you carry a headlamp; bring lights on your vehicles."],
	["Modules", "A module is a piece of a build saved on its own: a wing, an engine pod, a landing gear. Select the parts with the Transform tool, open Modules (beside Saves), name it and press Save selection. Place puts one into any build: click where it goes, like a paste, then turn it with the Transform tool. Links between the module's own parts come with it. Modules are files in the modules folder inside the saves folder, in the same format as saves."],
	["Speed and settings", "If the game runs slowly, open Settings → Display and press \"Laptop (fast)\": it lowers the resolution to 70%, turns off edge smoothing and shadows, and draws trees out to 1.2 km. Each can also be set by hand. Physics always runs 240 steps a second, so a slow frame rate does not change how builds behave."],
]
